Once a weapon never misses, all the more reason to use a
level draining weapon against any monster not resistant.

I've read plenty of posts about comparing level drain with
points of damage and the more I read the more I think level
drain is more devastating than any of the discussed
equivalent damage numbers.  With level drain, if the monster
runs away and heals back to full strength, each blow struck
reduces that fully healed nastiness.

Softening up enemies as they approach with spells, missiles
and wands continues to work great, especially if said
monsters die.  A dead monster is a well softened one (except
a rubber chicken).  But by the time you're exploring Gehennom,
there isn't all that much chance to fight at range any more.
Melee gets very important down there even for a wizard or
other ranged spellcaster.

Even though plenty of monsters in Gehennom are level
drain resistant, having a weapon that does double damage
matters.  But then we've moved into a late-game discussion
not a middle-game discussion ...



